s it use it  and the answer of course is that yes it  does as I've stated already and it  doesn't in a pretty simple way in the  same way that the cloud does it it takes  each of the services that you see you  know from YouTube the photos takes these  same scalable building blocks and then  layers on top of all of their needs to  provide you know the level of  availability performance durability that  you're looking for in your storage  systems so this allows both Google  services and Google Cloud customers to  scale in the exact same way because  they're using the exact same set of  building blocks and so you know you can  have the confidence that if you go off  and watch Cobra Kai and there's also a  million other people that want to watch  Cobra Kai at the same time then these  three services are behind the scenes  scaling to meet the needs and  traditionally what's interesting is that  much of the use cases in Tuborg spanner  and Colossus and all of the innovation  in them was driven by the Google  services but as our cloud customers have  really scaled across the globe they are  now providing a lot of the innovation as  well so let's look at a typical flow  let's say a user launches a gke cluster  on to a series of servers so the VMS in  that cluster get launched onto a series  of the servers that they requested and  that's using Borg by the way and so then  these VMs running on those services in  the GK cluster access their favorite  storage services let's say they use  persistent disk for their boot volume  and then they used file store and Google  Cloud storage for their unstructured  data once they start accessing those  services the exact same big three  building blocks Colossus spanner and  Borg are underneath the covers providing  all of the services so Borg is  provisioning the resources for that  storage system spanner is storing the  metadata to say for example who can  access the data and where is the data  actually  gated and then colossus of course is  providing all storage for the data so  now that we understand these three basic  services for the rest of this talk let's  deep dive into cloud storage and how it  makes use of Colossus so from the user  point of view you know Google has spent  a lot of time and effort to try to make  cloud storage as easy to use as possible  you know we want it to be as easy as  Dylan up and emptying out and actual  byte but behind the scenes from Google's  perspective cloud storage is actually a  massive distributed system that has the  potential and take advantage of all of  our region's networks points of presence  and all the servers and disks that I  talked about earlier and it has to make  use of all those aspects to satisfy  three primary use cases content storage  compute analytics and machine learning  as well as backup and archiving and from  the cloud perspective we have lots of  customers in each of these areas that  are scaling using the services so for  example box is delivering a lot of  content to its users the Broad Institute  is constantly analyzing lots of  different genomes every day and the New  York Times you can find a really  interesting article about how it's using  Google Cloud Storage to archive its  entire photo history but Google Cloud  Storage can scale to meet these needs  because it's already been meeting the  needs of our Google cloud services such  as services such as YouTube ads and  photos which archives a lot the photos  so in this talk we've learned about how  Colossus is underpinning Google Cloud  storage and it provides support for  three very unique use cases and now I'm  going to turn it over to Dennis so he  can explain how it is Colossus achieve  all of this thanks Dean so we talked a  lot about GCS and how it can support a  very wide range of use cases from  archival storage to high-throughput  analytics but we don't talk a lot about  the system that lie  under GCSE Colossus which enables GCS is  unique capabilities so I'm gonna dive  into the details of how GCSE uses  Colossus and how Colossus works we'll  start from the top and work our way down  so I'm starting with the client library  which is how GCS interacts with Colossus  actually the client is probably the most  complex part of the system there's a lot  of functionality that goes directly in  there  for instance software raid and  applications can choose their own  encodings based on a platonic to fine  tune the performance cost trade-offs  that they need to make for different  workloads the foundation of Colossus is  its scalable metadata service called the  curator it scales because clients talk  to it only for control operations for  instance creating a file and because it  can scale out horizontally it can also  scale massively because it's built on  top of a no SQL database called like  BigTable in fact the original mode of  Google had for building Colossus was  that search was running into scaling  limits as Dean mentioned of its  predecessor the Google file system  putting filesystem metadata into  BigTable was the key trick that allowed  Colossus to scale up by over a hundred x  over the largest GFS clusters another  way the system scales is that Colossus  minimizes the number of hops for data on  the network data flows directly between  D servers which is are really not very  clever name for our simple network  detached disks and the Colossus client  finally Colossus includes background  storage managers called  custodians which handle things like disk  space balancing and raid reconstruction  they play a key role in maintaining the  durability and availability of data as  well as ensuring the storage system is  working  efficiently so that's Colossus in a  nutshell here's a picture of what a  typical Google cluster looks like the  key ingredient is having the shared  storage pool managed by the Colossus  control plane this large disaggregated  storage layer is able to support a wide  variety of applications on the same  underlying physical resources and each  application has the rough illusion of  having an isolated filesystem in  aggregate we're talking about really big  scale here a single cluster is scalable  to the exabytes of storage with tens of  thousands of machines and what I've  drawn here represents a typical mix of  internal Google services you've got some  YouTube serving and maybe some ads  mapreduces together with GCP VM  instances using GCS all together sharing  the same underlying Colossus file system  so it's really the case that the same  storage system that's driving these  global scale Google applications is also  driving our customers cloud workloads  the scale disaggregation of resources  and variety of workloads is the key to  driving efficiency and lowering costs  for instance we can provision to the  peak demand of low latency workloads  like maybe this YouTube serving workload  here and then run batch workloads like  mapreduces and other analytics more  cheaply by having them fill in the gaps  of otherwise idle time another great  thing about Colossus is it abstracts  away a ton of complexity that storage  intensive applications would otherwise  have to deal with for one thing frankly  our data centers have a tremendous  variety of underlying storage hardware  when we're adding capacity to the system  we're always buying the disk of the day  which is whatever disk currently has the  lowest total cost of ownership and the  same goes for flash and these devices  last a long time which means that each  cluster will have many disk sizes and  types add to that the  that applications have different  requirements around durability  availability and latency Colossus  addresses that by providing service  tiers and allowing applications to  specify erasure codes tuned to their  needs and add to that at Google scale  some hardware is failing virtually all  the time that's just a testament to the  scale that we're operating at it's not  like the hardware is unreliable it's  just that there's a lot of it so dealing  with some failures is the common case  Colossus tears i/o around these failures  and does fast background recovery to  provide highly durable storage within  the cluster despite all of this chaos  the end result is that for GCS as well  as Google's own services these Hardware  resources and their associated  complexity are totally abstracted away  systems provision bytes and i/o as  abstract undifferentiated units that  makes using storage very simple for all  the applications were running so now  let's deep dive into how Colossus helps  ensure the storage pool is being managed  efficiently I mentioned that  applications built on top of Colossus  can provision bytes and i/o as abstract  resources but as you might imagine it  involves a lot of magic to ensure that  all the i/o and all the bytes are  available when applications need them  but also not over provision either of  these things because that would mean a  lot of waste under the hood we take  advantage of the fact that data has a  wide variety of temperatures as we're  trying to illustrate here we mix in  flash not always to lower latency but  simply because flash is the most  efficient way to serve really hot data  if we do our job right we buy just  enough flash to push the i/o density per  gigabyte into for the remaining data  into the range that can be served by  disks and then we buy just enough disks  so that we can fill them all up if we do  this well  and have the right mix we end up with  really efficient storage now let's talk  about how colossus maximizes storage  efficiency for disk-based storage within  our population of disks as I mentioned  earlier we're gonna have a wide variety  of disk sizes to maximize efficiency  we'll want to do two things we'll want  to keep the disks full cuz empty bytes  are just excess inventory and we want to  keep the disks busy so we're not wasting  that disk spindle because each cluster  has this roughly the same IO bandwidth  what this means is that sorry because  each disk has roughly the same IO  bandwidth what this means is that we'll  have to keep hotter data evenly spread  across all the drives in the cluster and  then we just fill up all the remaining  space on each drive with cold bytes to  make this work the key problem that  Colossus has solved is placing data  intelligently turns out newly written  data is typically hotter so Colossus  spreads new writes evenly across all the  disks leading to a pretty even balance  of hot data and then Colossus as  background rebalance er moves older cold  data to bigger drives this works great  for analytics workloads because  analytics data typically cools off as it  ages now a lot of this stuff was  developed and refined over the years so  that Google can run its own services  search ads YouTube etc all those  services that Dean was talking about at  massive scale as simply and efficiently  as possible  but for cloud this means that cloud  users can also leverage this  functionality and you end up with simple  and inexpensive storage okay so this is  a picture of one of our internal  dashboards used to monitor and debug  storage performance in a cluster there's  a lot of boxes in this visualization  each of those small boxes is a D server  and their size by dis capacity and  colored by spindle utilization  looks like this we're really happy  basically the whole cluster is really  busy that's why it's looking all red  like that so we're getting all the value  out of our disk spindles sure there's  some darker color sprinkled in there but  it turns out those are d servers that  are down for maintenance are being  decommissioned  so that's Colossus in a nutshell  now let's pop the stack and talk about  GCS itself which is really just a layer  on top it turns out GCS is employing a  lot of the same tricks that Colossus  uses just at the region and multi region  level to illustrate how they're similar  here's a simple representation of a GCS  deployment in one of our DCP regions in  this example we've got three clusters  each with a GCS instance on top of  Colossus storage first off GCS employs  some of the same hot data placement  techniques used by Colossus placing data  close to where it's likely to be read  and also caching data to minimize  network distance and lower latency and  just like Colossus is rebalancing colder  data within the cluster GCS rebalances  colder data between clusters in the  region to make sure we're able to fully  utilize all available space as well as  ensuring that data is always durable and  available in the face of disruptions or  failures in the underlying  infrastructure GCS kind of thinks of  each cluster in Google's fleet as an  abstract disk and by managing these  clusters intelligently it's able to  handle all the diverse workloads that  users bring to GCS like content serving  and our archival and analytics and do it  efficiently the way we think of GCS is  that it's Colossus in the cloud so to  wrap up we know about GCP scalability  what we uncovered today is that GCP can  scale because it's built on top of  core Google services that are battle  tested and have demonstrated the ability  to scale to pretty massive levels in  fact GCS is just a layer on top of  Colossus which is the cluster level file  system that doesn't just handle the  storage needs of GCE VMs GCS content  serving etc but also handles all of  Google's internal storage needs from  search to Maps YouTube photos etc and  lastly we learned that there's a ton of  complexity under the covers of GCP but  systems like Colossus have abstracted  all of that away allowing GCP customers  to just focus on their business so to  wrap up I want to give a big thanks to  Dean for being my partner in crime for  this deep dive into what lies under the  hood of storage at TCP hey Dennis glad  to be that partner and thanks a ton for  sharing your extremely deep knowledge in  Colossus and Google Cloud storage and I  really hope this gives our customers  confidence that by using this skill  underlying scalable infrastructure that  they can scale their applications as big  as they're looking for thanks a lot I  hope you found this as interesting as we  do and that you learned a lot have a  great day

